WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.822
Interval dynamic programming solves problems where a sequence is reduced by merging or splitting contiguous ranges.

00:00:07.822 --> 00:00:15.167
Because wide intervals depend on narrower ones, the outer loop must iterate over increasing interval length.

00:00:15.167 --> 00:00:20.200
Fixing length and left endpoint determines the right endpoint immediately.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:27.398
To evaluate interval L to R, iterate a split point k across all positions between L and R.

00:00:27.398 --> 00:00:35.982
Combine the optimal solutions for the left sub-interval and right sub-interval plus the cost of the final merge.

00:00:35.982 --> 00:00:42.420
Three nested loops yield a cubic O(N cubed) runtime suited for N up to four hundred.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:50.115
In palindrome and bracket deletion problems, check whether the outer characters at L and R can pair together.

00:00:50.115 --> 00:00:56.713
Matching endpoints let you transition directly from the inner interval L plus one to R minus one.

00:00:56.713 --> 00:01:01.951
Take the best result across both endpoint pairing and mid-interval splitting.

00:01:02.233 --> 00:01:10.746
When items sit on a circular ring rather than a straight line, concatenate the array with itself to form a sequence of length 2N.

00:01:10.746 --> 00:01:15.827
Run standard interval DP on segments up to length N across the doubled array.

00:01:15.827 --> 00:01:20.644
The optimal answer over all length-N windows solves the circular problem.
